---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { discoveries, pageUrl } from '../data/discoveries';
const images = import.meta.glob('../../assets/*.png', {eager:true, import:'default'});
---
<Layout title="Ten ideas at a changing frontier" description="Explore ten AI-assisted mathematical advances from 2025–2026 through illustrated explanations and hands-on experiments.">
<section class="hero"><div class="hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">AN ILLUSTRATED ATLAS · 10 DISCOVERIES</p><h1>Mathematics has<br/>a new <em>research partner.</em></h1><p class="lede">In 2025, AI helped find more economical calculations and unexpected arrangements. In 2026, the story expanded to longstanding conjectures, new proofs, and the machinery of verification.</p><p>The astonishing part is the range. A single period now connects questions about packing balls, coloring networks, prime numbers, and the limits of fluid equations.</p><a class="button" href="#atlas">Explore the ten ideas <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div><a class="hero-plate" href={pageUrl('cycle-double-cover')} aria-label="Explore every connection, exactly twice"><span class="eyebrow">FROM THE ATLAS / PLATE 05</span><Image src={images['../../assets/05-cycle-double-cover.png'] as any} alt={discoveries[4].alt} width={700} format="webp" loading="eager"/><span class="plate-caption">A small network. A remarkably general promise. <b>↗</b></span></a></section>
<section class="context-band" id="purpose"><div><p class="eyebrow">WHY THIS GUIDE EXISTS</p><h2>You do not need to follow a proof<br/>to appreciate <em>what changed.</em></h2></div><div><p>Each page gives you one idea to hold onto: an image, a plain-language explanation, and a small experiment. Follow the timeline or open whichever question catches your eye.</p><p>These are ten selected milestones, not a ranking or a complete history. Their sources include <a href={discoveries[0].source}>DeepMind’s 2025 results</a>, <a href={discoveries[5].source}>OpenAI’s 2026 research</a>, and <a href={discoveries[8].source}>Anthropic’s formalization work</a>. The <a href={pageUrl('navier-stokes')}>September Navier–Stokes announcement</a> closes this snapshot of a rapidly moving field.</p></div></section>
<section class="reading-key"><p class="eyebrow">FOUR WAYS MATHEMATICS MOVES FORWARD</p><div class="key-grid"><article><span>01 / FIND</span><h3>A construction</h3><p>Exhibit an arrangement or procedure that works.</p></article><article><span>02 / LIMIT</span><h3>A bound</h3><p>Strengthen a guarantee without claiming the whole answer.</p></article><article><span>03 / SETTLE</span><h3>A proof or disproof</h3><p>Establish a claim, or show why it cannot hold.</p></article><article><span>04 / CHECK</span><h3>A formalization</h3><p>Make a proof’s deductions explicit enough for a checker.</p></article></div></section>
<section id="atlas" class="atlas"><div class="section-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE ATLAS</p><h2>Ten windows onto discovery.</h2></div><p>Start anywhere.<br/>Every plate opens an experiment.</p></div>{[2025,2026].map(year=><div class="year-group"><div class="year-heading"><span>{year}</span><p>{year===2025?'The groundwork: new procedures, new possibilities.':'The acceleration: a much wider mathematical frontier.'}</p></div><div class="atlas-grid">{discoveries.filter(d=>d.year===year).map(d=><a class="discovery-card" href={pageUrl(d.slug)}><div class="card-image"><Image src={images[`../../assets/${d.image}`] as any} alt={d.alt} width={520} format="webp" loading="lazy"/><span class="plate-number">{String(discoveries.indexOf(d)+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span></div><div class="card-body"><div class="card-meta"><span>{d.date}</span><span>{d.type}</span></div><h3>{d.title}</h3><p>{d.idea}</p><div class="card-bottom"><span>{d.field}</span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></div></div></a>)}</div></div>)}</section>
<section class="closing"><p class="eyebrow">KEEP THE WONDER. KEEP THE DISTINCTIONS.</p><h2>A changing way to do mathematics.</h2><p>The milestones suggest an expanding role for AI in proposing, exploring, and checking mathematical arguments. They also make human judgment more visible: choosing the questions, establishing what a claim actually says, checking its support, and explaining why it matters.</p><p>This guide links back to the research. “Announced proof” identifies a release, not a promise that every subsequent review is complete. The diagrams are invitations to understand the questions.</p><a class="text-link" href={pageUrl(discoveries[0].slug)}>Begin with the first discovery →</a></section>
</Layout>
